The code statements that lead to the bug are:
u32 log_start = only_new_msgs ? READ_ONCE(log->read_index) : 0;
u32 log_end = READ_ONCE(log->write_index);
Reason:
These statements read the read and write indices directly from firmware-supplied data without any validation. Since the firmware can provide arbitrary values, the indices may exceed the buffer size (data_size). When these unchecked indices are later used as offsets in `fw_log_print_lines` (e.g., `log_data + log_start`), they can cause out-of-bounds memory access, leading to potential memory corruption or information disclosure.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (log_start >= data_size)
	log_start = 0;
if (log_end > data_size)
	log_end = data_size;
Reason:
These added bounds checks clamp the read and write indices to safe values within the buffer limits. If the firmware supplies an out-of-bounds read index, it is reset to 0; if the write index exceeds data_size, it is capped to data_size. This prevents the subsequent buffer offset calculations from accessing memory outside the allocated log buffer, eliminating the risk of out-of-bounds access.

